\description{
Group sequential counterpart of \code{\link[=graph_optimise]{graph_optimise()}}. Optimise the hypothesis
weights and transition matrix of a graph-based multiple testing procedure
for a design with several analyses, using \code{\link[nloptr:nloptr]{nloptr::nloptr()}} local
optimisation and, optionally, a genetic algorithm using \code{\link[GA:ga]{GA::ga()}} for
global optimisation.
}
\details{
The output is a graph where a specified objective function - the
\emph{trial success measure} - is maximised under given constraints on the graph
structure, conditional on the repeated p-values supplied. Because the gain
sees the analysis at which each hypothesis was rejected, an early rejection
can be worth more than a late one.

The search, the encoding and the pruning are those of
\code{\link[=graph_optimise]{graph_optimise()}}. Only the evaluation changes: the group sequential
kernel runs the fixed-sample cascade once per analysis, carrying the
updated graph forward, and reports the analysis at which each hypothesis
was declared rejected. Those decision times are what \code{trial_success}
scores.

With a stopping rule the kernel stops each simulated trial at the first
interim whose condition holds, so the gain and every reported power see
only the rejections made before the trial stopped; a trial that rejects
every hypothesis stops at that analysis in any case.

\strong{Optimised spending parameters.} When \code{pvals} is a spending spec, from
\code{\link[=transform_pvalues_gsd]{transform_pvalues_gsd()}} with one or more \code{\link[=sf_hsd_opt]{sf_hsd_opt()}} entries, the
Hwang-Shih-DeCani parameter of each tagged group is chosen jointly with
the graph. Every value the search can try is a node of the parameter's
lattice (the multiples of \code{step} inside \code{bounds}), and every node's
boundary table was built and checked for well ordering when the spec was
created, so the chosen design controls the error rate as any fixed choice
would. The search has two stages:
\itemize{
\item the global stage runs the genetic algorithm on the graph genes with one
gene per spending parameter appended, each mapped to a lattice node, on
the \code{nsim_global} subsample. The repeated p-values of that subsample are
computed once at every node. The default population size counts each
parameter as one more hypothesis;
\item the local stage refines the graph by COBYLA at the genetic algorithm's
parameter values, then walks each parameter's lattice outward from
there and re-optimises the graph at every node, starting COBYLA from
the best of the current optimum's graph, the previous node's graph and
the best graph the genetic algorithm found at that node (a
\emph{warm-started profile}). It moves to the best node; with two or more
parameters the sweep is repeated until no parameter moves, at most
three rounds. Every gain of this stage is computed on one ordering of
the simulated trials, so differences between nodes are paired.
}

The profile is returned as \code{spending_profile}: for every node of each
parameter, with the other parameters held at their chosen values, the
gain with the graph re-optimised (\code{gain}), the gain of the chosen graph
kept fixed (\code{gain_fixed_graph}), the paired difference from the optimum
(\code{diff}) and its batch-means standard error over 100 blocks of trials
(\code{se}). At the chosen value \code{gain} equals \code{gain_fixed_graph} and \code{diff}
and \code{se} are 0. \code{print()} condenses the profile to two \emph{plateaus}: the
connected run of nodes around the chosen value whose \code{diff} is at most
\code{5e-4}, and the same run for the fixed-graph gain, measured from its
largest value. A wide plateau means the parameter hardly matters for this
design within that range; the fixed-graph plateau is narrower when the
best graph depends on the parameter. \code{summary()} prints the table and
the number of nodes the genetic algorithm visited.

As on the fixed path, the better of the two stages' graphs is kept, each
scored on the full sample at its own parameter values. If the genetic
algorithm's graph is kept, its parameter values are reported and the
profile is still the local stage's, which started from them. Unlike the
fixed path, a \code{start_graph} is honoured when \code{global_search = FALSE}.

The parameters and the graph are chosen on the same simulated trials that
score them, so the reported gain is optimistic (the winner's curse), a
little more so than for the graph alone. To check the design, simulate
fresh p-values, build a spec from them with the same \code{spending}, and
evaluate the chosen graph with \code{\link[=calc_power_pvals_gsd]{calc_power_pvals_gsd()}} on
\code{resolve_pvalues_gsd(new_spec, result$spending_param)}.

The boundary tables are the main cost of building a spec: about 0.9 s per
lattice node at the default \code{grid_size = 1024} with two analyses, about
20 s for the default 21-node lattice. \code{grid_size = 512} halves this; in
the package's measurements its largest relative interpolation error
against \code{grid_size = 1024} was \code{8.2e-6}. A spec can be reused across
gains and stopping rules on the same draws.
}
\examples{
# two hypotheses, an interim at 50\% information and a final analysis
corr <- matrix(c(1, 0.5, 0.5, 1), nrow = 2)

set.seed(1)
raw <- simulate_pvalues_gsd(
    power_nominal = c(0.9, 0.8),
    corr_matrix = corr,
    info_frac = c(0.5, 1),
    nsim = 2000
)

pvals <- transform_pvalues_gsd(
    raw,
    spending = gsDesign::sfLDOF,
    grid_size = 128L
)

# a rejection at the final analysis is worth 75\% of one at the interim
gain <- trial_success_gsd(
    r1 * d(t1) + r2 * d(t2),
    d = c(1, 0.75)
)

\donttest{
result <- graph_optimise_gsd(
    pvals = pvals,
    graph_constraint = graph_constraint_free(2),
    trial_success = gain,
    stopping_rule = stopping_rule_gsd(r1),
    num_threads = 2
)

# choose the spending parameter of H2 jointly with the graph
spec <- transform_pvalues_gsd(
    raw,
    spending = list(gsDesign::sfLDOF, sf_hsd_opt(bounds = c(-4, 0))),
    grid_size = 128L
)
result_spending <- graph_optimise_gsd(
    pvals = spec,
    graph_constraint = graph_constraint_free(2),
    trial_success = gain,
    stopping_rule = stopping_rule_gsd(r1),
    num_threads = 2
)
result_spending$spending_param
result_spending
}
}
